Document.description_en:
To ensure Ottawa&#39;s south west region has the power to grow, Hydro Ottawa Limited (Hydro Ottawa) will construct a new 27.6 kV municipal transformer station on Greenbank Road, approximately 450 metres south of the Hunt Club Road intersection. The station, to be constructed within the National Capital Region Greenbelt, will serve existing customers and support projected growth in electricity demand for new residential and commercial developments in south Nepean for many years to come. The Station will enhance reliability, reduce the size and frequency of outages, and provide redundancy to the area&#39;s electricity system in the event of future extreme weather events. The Greenbank municipal transformer station is one of many future-planning investment projects led by Hydro Ottawa to support growth and grid resilience, and to make the region&#39;s electricity system as sustainable as possible.

The site is located directly adjacent to the existing Hydro One 230kV transmission corridor. This proximity simplifies the interconnection process and improves overall system reliability.&nbsp; The site is approximately 1.6 hectares (ha) in size and is located immediately west of Greenbank Road, approximately 460 m south of West Hunt Club Road in Ottawa, Ontario.&nbsp; The Site is located in the northeast corner of the CFIA&#39;s Ottawa Laboratory, which encompasses approximately 837 ha between Fallowfield Road to the south and Hunt Club Road to the north. The Site is situated within agricultural fields, with relatively small tree stands to the south and a surface water feature (tributary to Graham Creek) to the west. The zoning of the Site is AG (Agricultural).

The Site is bordered by:


	Agricultural lands and meadows and West Hunt Club Rod to the north
	Greenbank Road and agricultural lands belonging to the National Capital Commission to the east
	Agricultural lands, small tree stands, and a drainage feature on CFIA property to the south
	Agricultural ands, a treed tributary corridor and Cedarview Road to the west.


&nbsp;



Figure 1: Site map showing the existing Hydro One transmission lines and the proposed location of the new station

&nbsp;

The Project is anticipated to be completed between Fall 2026 and Spring 2028 and will consist of the following components:


	The construction of a single-level building with a basement (approximately 17 m &times; 25 m) to house critical electrical equipment, including switchgear, fuses, and protection and control systems;
	The development of an open-air electrical yard (approximately 53 m &times; 61 m) including the installation of two transformers, two high-voltage breakers, two high-voltage disconnect switches, and associated equipment mounted on concrete foundations;
	The installation of two firewalls integrated into the electrical yard design to provide visual screening of the transformers;
	The construction of a 6-metre-wide private access road connecting the site to Greenbank Road;
	Site preparation activities, including excavation, installation of geotechnical piles, establishment of a staging area, installation of a security fence, and construction of a parking area;
	Landscaping works, including tree planting, installation of vegetative buffers, and construction of an earth berm along the site frontage; and
	The installation of approximately 4.8 kilometres of underground electrical infrastructure to support the new Greenbank Station and surrounding areas. Including:
	
		duct bank system beneath the existing multi-use pathway along Greenbank Road to be completed in coordination with the City of Ottawa pathway repairs. 
		Install 8.5Mx12m switchgear unit at North-West corner of Greenbank and Fallowfield rd.&nbsp; 
		The underground electrical infrastructure will extend westward for approximately 1.2 kilometres along the north side of Fallowfield Road, where it will connect to the main CFIA campus.
	
	


Public Services and Procurement Canada (PSPC) will act as the lead federal authority for the Project on federal lands. In addition to PSPC, the Project will be reviewed under the Impact Assessment Act by the Canadian Food Inspection Agency (CFIA), in its role as landowner and authorizing authority, and by the National Capital Commission (NCC), as the federal authority responsible for issuing approvals under the National Capital Act.

Document.description_fr:
Pour veiller &agrave; ce que le secteur sud-ouest d&#39;Ottawa dispose de l&#39;&eacute;nergie n&eacute;cessaire pour soutenir sa croissance, Hydro Ottawa limit&eacute;e (Hydro Ottawa) va construire un nouveau poste de transformation municipal de 27,6&nbsp;kV sur le chemin Greenbank, &agrave; environ 450&nbsp;m&egrave;tres au sud de l&#39;intersection avec le chemin Hunt Club. &Eacute;rig&eacute; dans la ceinture de verdure de la r&eacute;gion de la capitale nationale, le poste Greenbank desservira des clients actuels et soutiendra la hausse pr&eacute;vue de la demande d&#39;&eacute;lectricit&eacute; attribuable &agrave; de nouveaux d&eacute;veloppements r&eacute;sidentiels et commerciaux &agrave; Nepean-Sud, et ce, pendant de nombreuses ann&eacute;es. Le poste va am&eacute;liorer la fiabilit&eacute;, r&eacute;duire l&#39;&eacute;tendue et la fr&eacute;quence des pannes, et assurer une redondance dans le r&eacute;seau d&#39;&eacute;lectricit&eacute; du secteur en cas de ph&eacute;nom&egrave;nes m&eacute;t&eacute;orologiques extr&ecirc;mes. Le poste de transformation municipal Greenbank fait partie d&#39;une s&eacute;rie de nombreux projets d&#39;investissement porteurs d&#39;avenir men&eacute;s par Hydro Ottawa pour soutenir la croissance et la r&eacute;silience du r&eacute;seau et le rendre aussi durable que possible.

L&#39;emplacement est adjacent &agrave; l&#39;actuel corridor de transport de 230&nbsp;kV d&#39;Hydro One. Cette proximit&eacute; simplifiera le processus d&#39;interconnexion et am&eacute;liorera la fiabilit&eacute; g&eacute;n&eacute;rale du r&eacute;seau. L&#39;emplacement est d&#39;une superficie approximative de 1,6&nbsp;hectare (ha) et est situ&eacute; imm&eacute;diatement &agrave; l&#39;ouest du chemin Greenbank, environ 460&nbsp;m au sud du chemin Hunt Club Ouest &agrave; Ottawa, en Ontario. L&#39;emplacement est &eacute;galement situ&eacute; &agrave; l&#39;angle nord-est du Laboratoire d&#39;Ottawa de l&#39;ACIA, qui s&#39;&eacute;tend sur environ 837&nbsp;ha entre le chemin Fallowfield au sud et le chemin Hunt Club au nord. L&#39;emplacement se trouve sur des terres agricoles qui comprennent des bosquets d&#39;arbres relativement petits au sud ainsi qu&#39;un &eacute;l&eacute;ment hydrographique de surface (un affluent du ruisseau Graham) &agrave; l&#39;ouest. Le zonage de l&#39;emplacement est AG (agricole).

L&#39;emplacement est bord&eacute; par&nbsp;:


	Des terres agricoles et des prairies ainsi que par le chemin Hunt Club Ouest au nord;
	Le chemin Greenbank et des terres agricoles appartenant &agrave; la Commission de la capitale nationale &agrave; l&#39;est;
	Des terres agricoles, des petits bosquets et un syst&egrave;me de drainage sur le terrain de l&#39;ACIA au sud;
	Des terres agricoles, un corridor affluent bois&eacute; et le chemin Cedarview &agrave; l&#39;ouest.


&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

Legend

Orange -&gt; Couloir de transport actuel

Blue -&gt; Emplacement du PTM propos&eacute;

Sch&eacute;ma 1&nbsp;: Carte indiquant les lignes de transport d&#39;Hydro One actuelles et l&#39;emplacement propos&eacute; pour le nouveau poste de transformation

&nbsp;

Le projet devrait &ecirc;tre r&eacute;alis&eacute; entre l&#39;automne 2026 et le printemps 2028. Il comprendra les &eacute;tapes suivantes&nbsp;:


	La construction d&#39;un b&acirc;timent d&#39;un seul &eacute;tage avec un sous-sol (environ 17&nbsp;m x 25&nbsp;m) permettant d&#39;accueillir de l&#39;appareillage &eacute;lectrique essentiel, y compris des dispositifs de commutation, des fusibles et des syst&egrave;mes de protection et de commande;
	L&#39;am&eacute;nagement d&#39;un enclos &agrave; ciel ouvert pour l&#39;&eacute;quipement &eacute;lectrique (environ 53&nbsp;m x 61&nbsp;m) comprenant l&#39;installation de deux transformateurs, deux disjoncteurs &agrave; haute tension, deux sectionneurs d&#39;alimentation &agrave; haute tension et des dispositifs connexes, le tout mont&eacute; sur des bases en b&eacute;ton;
	L&#39;installation de deux murs coupe-feu int&eacute;gr&eacute;s &agrave; la conception de l&#39;enclos afin de prot&eacute;ger les transformateurs;
	La construction d&#39;une voie d&#39;acc&egrave;s priv&eacute;e &ndash; d&#39;une largeur de 6&nbsp;m&egrave;tres &ndash; reliant l&#39;emplacement au chemin Greenbank;
	Des activit&eacute;s de pr&eacute;paration de l&#39;emplacement, y compris de l&#39;excavation, l&#39;installation de fondations g&eacute;otechniques, l&#39;&eacute;tablissement d&#39;une aire de stockage temporaire, l&#39;installation d&#39;une cl&ocirc;ture de s&eacute;curit&eacute; et l&#39;am&eacute;nagement d&#39;un stationnement;
	Des travaux d&#39;am&eacute;nagement paysager, notamment la plantation d&#39;arbres, l&#39;installation de bandes de v&eacute;g&eacute;taux et l&#39;am&eacute;nagement d&#39;un talus de terre le long de la fa&ccedil;ade de l&#39;emplacement;
	L&#39;installation d&#39;environ 4,8&nbsp;kilom&egrave;tres d&#39;infrastructures &eacute;lectriques souterraines destin&eacute;es &agrave; soutenir le nouveau poste Greenbank et les secteurs environnants. Entre autres&nbsp;:
	
		Un r&eacute;seau de canalisations en dessous de la piste multifonctionnelle situ&eacute;e le long du chemin Greenbank, &agrave; r&eacute;aliser en coordination avec la r&eacute;fection de la piste pr&eacute;vue par la Ville d&#39;Ottawa.
		Une installation de commutation (8,5&nbsp;m x 12&nbsp;m) &agrave; l&#39;angle nord-ouest du chemin Greenbank et du chemin Fallowfield.
		Les infrastructures &eacute;lectriques souterraines s&#39;&eacute;tendront &agrave; l&#39;ouest sur une distance d&#39;environ 1,2&nbsp;kilom&egrave;tre le long du c&ocirc;t&eacute; nord du chemin Fallowfield, o&ugrave; elles seront raccord&eacute;es au campus principal de l&#39;ACIA.
	
	


Services publics et Approvisionnement Canada (SPAC) agira comme l&#39;autorit&eacute; f&eacute;d&eacute;rale principale relativement aux terres f&eacute;d&eacute;rales faisant partie du projet. En plus de SPAC, le projet fera l&#39;objet d&#39;un examen en vertu de la Loi sur l&#39;&eacute;valuation d&#39;impact par l&#39;Agence canadienne d&#39;inspection des aliments (ACIA), en sa qualit&eacute; de propri&eacute;taire foncier et d&#39;autorit&eacute; comp&eacute;tente, et par la Commission de la capitale nationale (CCN), en sa qualit&eacute; d&#39;autorit&eacute; f&eacute;d&eacute;rale responsable d&#39;&eacute;mettre des approbations en vertu de la Loi sur la capitale nationale.

